# 3. Separate Expected-Input Validation From Intent Switching

This is one change I strongly recommend over the current proposal.

Do NOT make one giant function responsible for everything:

```python
check_intent_switch(
    message,
    current_flow,
    expected_shape,
    current_node,
    buttons,
    ...
)
```

That will become difficult to reason about.

Instead use:

```python
expected = validate_expected_input(
    node=current_node,
    user_input=user_input,
    state=state,
)

if expected.valid:
    return handle_expected_input(...)
```

Only if expected input validation fails:

```python
switch = detect_intent_switch(
    user_input=user_input,
    current_flow=state.active_flow,
)
```

Conceptually:

```text
USER INPUT
    │
    ▼
Does this satisfy what the current node expects?
    │
 ┌──┴──┐
YES    NO
 │      │
 ▼      ▼
normal  switch detector
logic       │
            ▼
       keyword / regex
            │
       ┌────┴────┐
      hit       miss
       │          │
       ▼          ▼
    reroute     LLM
                  │
              ┌───┴───┐
             clear   unclear
               │       │
               ▼       ▼
            reroute  reprompt
```

This directly fixes the false-positive problem from the second architecture note.

